This is a FIFA 2026 World Cup prediction project built in Python.

Project structure:
- src/data/ — data loading and cleaning
- src/features/ — feature engineering
- src/models/ — elo, xgboost, neural network, ensemble
- src/simulation/ — tournament simulator
- src/evaluation/ — validation
- tests/ — unit tests for each module

Coding standards:
- All functions must have docstrings
- All modules must have corresponding unit tests in tests/
- Use pandas for data manipulation
- Use numpy for numerical operations
- Team names must be standardized using clean_team_name() before any join
- Raw data in data/raw/ is never modified

Data notes:
- Primary match dataset: results.csv (home_team, away_team columns)
- Rankings dataset: fifa_rankings.csv (team column)
- Team names have unicode issues and inconsistencies across files
- All team name standardization handled in src/data/collect.py

Commenting standards:
- Every function must have a docstring explaining: what it does, 
  args, and return value. Use this format:

  def function_name(arg1, arg2):
      """
      Brief description of what the function does.
      
      Args:
          arg1: description
          arg2: description
          
      Returns:
          description of return value
      """

- Inline comments for anything non-obvious, not for obvious code
- Every file must have a top level comment explaining its purpose
- No commented out dead code — delete it instead
